Why should you get in trouble for helping your friends?

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:03.280
I do, Tom. You, uh, heard anything from Ed today?

00:43:04.910 --> 00:43:07.880
Not yet. I guess he'll be along any day now.

00:43:08.780 --> 00:43:13.680
Well, I checked the paperwork, and technically speaking, that bank examiner is right about your loan.

00:43:14.580 --> 00:43:21.140
And you and I know that loan is okay, but that nitpicking J. Edgar Todd is gonna look at it and say that it ain't on the up and up.

00:43:22.340 --> 00:43:23.560
What do you think's gonna happen, Bill?

00:43:24.340 --> 00:43:29.960
Well, if I don't have that money by noon tomorrow, I suppose it means I'm gonna get jumped on with both Uncle Sam and...

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:35.810
Don't worry, Bill. I'll raise money for you on time.

00:43:36.840 --> 00:43:38.520
I know you would if you could, Tom.

00:43:45.840 --> 00:43:50.300
What's Long Bill thinking? He is president of the First National Bank.

00:43:50.740 --> 00:43:55.340
There are government regulations and such. He had no right putting through such an improper loan.

00:43:57.010 --> 00:43:58.450
So, you gonna help me out, Coop?

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:07.980
Now, don't say no. I owe that money on a call loan. It's been called.

00:44:08.650 --> 00:44:13.970
And the man who called it is a man that I've slept on the same blanket with in cow camps for 10 years.

00:44:14.600 --> 00:44:18.690
Now, he's got to have that money, and I've got to get it for him.

00:44:18.940 --> 00:44:20.840
Well, I wish I could, Tom, but I have a partner, you know?

00:44:21.500 --> 00:44:26.720
And besides that, we're making a shipment of $15,000 to Maya Brothers in Rockdale to buy cotton.

00:44:27.330 --> 00:44:29.910
He goes out on the first coach tomorrow at dawn, so you see.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:31.640
That leaves our cash quite short.

00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:32.500
I see.

00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:34.000
Sorry we can't arrange it for you, Tom.

00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:04.540
Howdy, George.

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:06.400
Howdy, Tom.

00:45:08.270 --> 00:45:11.040
You know, George, I got a cattle deal going.

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:12.740
Gonna come through any day now.

00:45:13.020 --> 00:45:13.520
Not so.

00:45:14.980 --> 00:45:20.960
Yep, but you see, I need some capital to cover a loan I took out from Long Bill.

00:45:21.720 --> 00:45:21.920
Huh?

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:28.390
What say you front me $10,000, and I'll double that return for you in a few days?

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:32.120
ten thousand dollars.

00:45:35.320 --> 00:45:36.920
You don't have that kind of money?

00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:43.000
Long Bill in some sort of trouble, Tom?

00:45:44.820 --> 00:45:46.960
Once that dang bank examiner come to town

00:45:46.960 --> 00:45:49.860
and call them my loan. If I don't pay

00:45:49.860 --> 00:45:51.940
that money back, it's gonna be on Long Bill's head

00:45:51.940 --> 00:45:53.560
for giving it to me in the first place.

00:45:54.580 --> 00:45:56.740
We're sorry to hear Long Bill is in trouble with the

00:45:56.740 --> 00:45:57.040
government.

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:02.480
Amen.

00:47:53.680 --> 00:47:59.870
But first of all, I need to ask...

00:48:04.020 --> 00:48:08.860
Oakdale watched as Wanda Gilmore performed her heroic deed that day.

00:48:09.650 --> 00:48:11.560
But who were we really watching?

00:48:12.130 --> 00:48:17.030
Did we know we were watching Wanda Gilmore, secret landlord of downtown Oakdale?

00:48:17.310 --> 00:48:17.670
No.

00:48:18.390 --> 00:48:21.650
Does Wanda Gilmore rightfully own the Oakdale Chronicle?

00:48:21.890 --> 00:48:27.240
Did her father Giles Gilmore obtain the Chronicle by swindling its previous owner?

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:29.970
Could property snatching be a family tradition?

00:48:30.090 --> 00:48:32.910
Tune in tonight for the full story.

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:37.950
Now they're going after my father.

00:48:38.170 --> 00:48:39.530
What did I ever do to them?

00:48:41.490 --> 00:48:43.580
How did your father get the Chronicle, Wanda?

00:48:43.740 --> 00:48:45.520
No, no, no. I wasn't even born then.

00:48:47.750 --> 00:48:49.910
Next, they'll be saying my papers are forged.

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:54.960
I don't understand why they're doing this to Miss Gilmore.

00:48:55.600 --> 00:48:59.970
Well, Joe, there are journalists who think some things are more newsworthy.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:04.660
than others even if it might end up hurting someone wish we could do

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:10.370
something what could we do their TV station you'd be surprised what a few

00:49:10.370 --> 00:49:15.150
people can do especially when they know the truth what if Miss Gilmore really

00:49:15.150 --> 00:49:19.410
doesn't own the Chronicle maybe we need to do a little investigating of our own

00:49:25.580 --> 00:49:29.260
that paper goes down paper goes up

00:49:30.090 --> 00:49:32.530
Paper goes down! Paper goes up!

00:49:33.790 --> 00:49:36.420
I could do this all day! Paper goes up!

00:49:36.480 --> 00:49:38.180
Okay, Wishbone, quit fooling around.

00:49:38.440 --> 00:49:40.500
Aww, you guys get to have all the fun!

00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:43.750
Sam, Sam, move that little lever

00:49:43.750 --> 00:49:45.920
and watch what happens. Paper goes up!

00:49:46.470 --> 00:49:48.950
Paper goes down! Paper go-

00:49:48.950 --> 00:49:57.950
Sam? Find anything yet?

00:49:59.090 --> 00:49:59.650
Not really.

00:50:02.330 --> 00:50:09.210
Hey, wait. On May 22nd, 1932, the ownership changed from Skelton to Gilmore.

00:50:10.200 --> 00:50:14.820
There's nothing in this history book, either. We're really coming up empty, you know.

00:50:17.450 --> 00:50:21.790
I found the 25th anniversary special edition of the Chronicle. Maybe there's something useful in it.

00:50:25.530 --> 00:50:28.010
Come on, it's my turn! Paper goes up, paper goes down!

00:50:32.140 --> 00:50:34.080
Look, here's a picture of Ms. Gilmore's father.

00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:36.260
Who are all those other people?

00:50:37.270 --> 00:50:39.470
I'm not sure, but I know that's Ethan Johnstone.

00:50:40.080 --> 00:50:41.160
Well, that's Hank's grandfather.

00:50:41.940 --> 00:50:42.980
Maybe Hank can help us out.

00:50:45.410 --> 00:50:46.810
Is there a way to get a print out of this?

00:50:47.260 --> 00:50:47.580
Sure.

00:50:50.450 --> 00:50:50.850
Blinda!

00:50:51.480 --> 00:50:52.900
Oh, I'm so glad you're here.

00:50:53.380 --> 00:50:54.780
Tell us what's going on.

00:50:55.060 --> 00:50:56.640
We've been getting calls all day.

00:50:56.920 --> 00:50:58.660
What's this about you not owning the Chronicle?

00:50:58.860 --> 00:50:59.940
How are we supposed to respond?

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:04.380
of these allegations? Please. What do you want us to say? Everyone, there's nothing to worry about.

00:51:04.560 --> 00:51:16.000
Let's get back to work. Wanda, this is a story. We're going to have to run something in the paper

00:51:16.000 --> 00:51:25.680
about it. I need to ask you, do you own the Chronicle or don't you? Well, of course I own

00:51:25.680 --> 00:51:29.970
the paper, Jenny. Please, I just need some time to think this through.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:33.190
Ms. Gilmore, is there any way that I can help?

00:51:33.770 --> 00:51:34.550
Thanks, Hank.

00:51:35.050 --> 00:51:36.730
Now, I'm sure this will all get straightened out

00:51:36.730 --> 00:51:38.530
and we can go back to the way things used to be.

00:51:40.660 --> 00:51:42.600
I'm afraid that can't happen.

00:51:43.480 --> 00:51:45.400
Leon King, what do you want?

00:51:46.450 --> 00:51:51.450
Oh, Wanda, please. Look, for you.

00:51:52.960 --> 00:51:55.160
A cyclamen? Well, how beautiful.

00:51:55.710 --> 00:51:57.190
Well, I know how much you love plants,

00:51:57.210 --> 00:51:59.790
and I thought this would be a fitting going away gift.

00:52:01.440 --> 00:52:02.420
Who's going away?

00:52:03.670 --> 00:52:05.290
Oh, you are.

00:52:06.970 --> 00:52:13.070
You see, I am now rightful owner of the Oakdale Chronicle.

00:52:13.150 --> 00:52:16.610
What are you talking about?

00:52:17.670 --> 00:52:18.890
I did a little checking one.

00:52:19.770 --> 00:52:25.920
According to city records, the Oakdale Chronicle building is still listed in the name of Abel Skelton.

00:52:27.230 --> 00:52:29.970
All I had to do was find Skelton's living room.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:33.140
and buy it from him. Legally buy it, I might add.

00:52:33.490 --> 00:52:37.160
Now you look here. My father built this paper.

00:52:37.460 --> 00:52:40.820
Your father? Your father never owned the Oakdale Chronicle.

00:52:42.270 --> 00:52:44.570
No telling what kind of shady deal he made with Skelton.

00:52:45.790 --> 00:52:53.520
The beauty of it is that I wouldn't have known about the rightful ownership

00:52:54.530 --> 00:52:57.890
if you hadn't become Oakdale's newest hero.

00:52:59.630 --> 00:52:59.970
That m-

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:02.260
Mitch McCain is really something.

00:53:02.710 --> 00:53:04.450
He should be. I pay him enough.

00:53:05.690 --> 00:53:08.820
What? You own that TV station?

00:53:09.080 --> 00:53:10.240
As well as two others.

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:11.600
But don't worry, Wanda.

00:53:12.460 --> 00:53:14.980
Your paper is in the hands of an experienced newsman.

00:53:15.940 --> 00:53:20.370
Oh, I won't turn this paper over to your kind of journalism.

00:53:21.360 --> 00:53:22.820
There must be something I can do.

00:53:23.060 --> 00:53:25.090
Oh, there is one.

00:53:27.800 --> 00:53:28.840
Get out of my newspaper.

00:53:30.600 --> 00:53:31.920
You've got till the end of the day.

00:53:34.380 --> 00:53:36.500
I'll be back then and decide who else goes.

00:54:36.880 --> 00:54:37.940
Are you okay, Wanda?

00:54:38.660 --> 00:54:38.960
No.

00:54:39.240 --> 00:54:45.420
Is there anything I can do?

00:54:46.880 --> 00:54:47.760
I doubt it, Ellen.

00:54:48.320 --> 00:54:50.740
It's all a little bit late for anything, isn't it?

00:54:52.680 --> 00:54:53.940
No, it isn't, Wanda.

00:54:54.950 --> 00:54:56.490
It's never too late for your best friend.

00:54:57.580 --> 00:54:57.980
Thanks.

00:54:58.930 --> 00:54:59.850
It'll all work out.

00:55:01.180 --> 00:55:03.720
Because you're bigger than the newspaper, Wanda.

00:55:04.550 --> 00:55:09.400
And Wanda Gilmore is so much more than what a Mitch McCain shows us on TV.

00:55:12.600 --> 00:55:13.900
So sure anymore, Ellen?

00:55:15.420 --> 00:55:16.400
Not so sure.

00:56:19.230 --> 00:56:21.250
never needed to be fixed up this way, Tom.

00:56:21.470 --> 00:56:27.150
I was desperate, Bill.

00:56:28.880 --> 00:56:29.860
You called your loan.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:30.920
I had to answer you.

00:56:31.960 --> 00:56:33.020
You're a good friend, Tom.

00:56:33.550 --> 00:56:34.670
I admire your loyalty.

00:56:36.330 --> 00:56:39.840
When you think about all that money going out on the stage this morning,

00:56:40.020 --> 00:56:43.100
I didn't know what else to do.

00:56:44.080 --> 00:56:45.680
Well, something will work out.

00:56:52.800 --> 00:56:54.980
Tom, did you say that bank examiner was coming back?

00:56:55.490 --> 00:56:56.150
Around noon.

00:56:57.000 --> 00:56:57.620
What about Ed?

00:56:58.280 --> 00:56:59.880
Think he's on his way back with that money?

00:57:00.730 --> 00:57:02.890
He's got to be. There ain't no two ways about it.

00:57:03.660 --> 00:57:06.300
I suppose we could take us a ride into Montgomery, see if he's there.

00:57:08.210 --> 00:57:11.360
Sure. It'd be just like old times again, out on the trail.

00:57:11.760 --> 00:57:13.600
We had us some good times, didn't we, Tom?

00:57:14.400 --> 00:57:16.330
We sure did, Bill. We sure did.

00:57:21.920 --> 00:57:23.540
Dadgummit, Clive is at it again.

00:57:24.890 --> 00:57:27.150
Oh, bury me, no!

00:57:30.250 --> 00:57:31.780
It's all crap.

00:58:00.410 --> 00:58:06.360
Oh, bury me not on the lone prairie.

00:58:07.840 --> 00:58:29.640
You're the tumor.

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:34.460
up bendin' citizens of Trap-Rosa.

00:58:36.820 --> 00:58:38.840
Why don't you just do this town a favor

00:58:38.840 --> 00:58:41.420
and go crawl under a rock till you sleep this thing off?

00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:45.760
I don't have to take no orders from you, Tom Merlin.

00:58:47.470 --> 00:58:48.530
You two.

00:58:49.880 --> 00:58:53.480
You two run around here like you own this town.

00:58:53.860 --> 00:58:55.580
Now hold on just a minute.

00:58:55.760 --> 00:58:57.580
What's all this fuss about, Calliope?

00:58:58.060 --> 00:58:59.780
Just pure dang lucked it.

00:59:00.120 --> 00:59:02.740
You two are where you're at, and I'm where I'm at.

00:59:04.500 --> 00:59:06.640
With a different roll of the dice, why?

00:59:07.900 --> 00:59:09.580
Be wearing your boots right now.

00:59:10.480 --> 00:59:14.280
Well, if you just worked for once in your life, you would be wearing boots like mine.

00:59:15.230 --> 00:59:16.810
You see, that's what I just don't understand.

00:59:16.810 --> 00:59:18.870
How can a feller be so dang stupid?

00:59:19.250 --> 00:59:21.270
I was in a fighting mood, Tom.

00:59:23.530 --> 00:59:28.770
You know, I got a bullet right here with your name on it.

00:59:30.640 --> 00:59:34.540
Tom Merwin, I'm calling you out.

00:59:35.670 --> 00:59:36.610
He is out, Calliope.

00:59:39.020 --> 00:59:39.380
Okay.

00:59:41.750 --> 00:59:44.800
All right, Calliope, I'll take you up on that.

00:59:48.000 --> 00:59:50.890
Uh, why don't you call out the paces, Bill?

00:59:51.760 --> 00:59:54.140
Uh, oh, uh, sure thing, Tom.

01:00:03.480 --> 01:00:19.300
All right, boys, back to back. Here we go.

01:00:20.380 --> 01:00:28.600
One, two, three, four, five.

01:00:30.220 --> 01:00:35.140
Six, seven, eight, nine.

01:01:01.590 --> 01:01:04.900
What's the last time you make a fool out of me?

01:01:30.160 --> 01:01:48.680
I want you to stop this foolishness right now.

01:01:49.320 --> 01:01:54.820
Come inside, I'll make you a nice breakfast, okay?

01:01:55.080 --> 01:01:58.010
I guess that means you'll be having your breakfast somewhere else today.

01:02:03.610 --> 01:02:10.490
shot at Maine. He never shot at Maine before. Now he's asking for it. I sure am glad I found you two

01:02:10.490 --> 01:02:15.580
fellas. Buck, guess you're right about Calliope. Calliope's out there shooting up the whole town

01:02:15.580 --> 01:02:20.470
again, Buck. Now what are you going to do about it? I don't see that I got much of a choice.

01:02:22.460 --> 01:02:25.420
Raise your right hand. He's kidding, right?

01:02:31.630 --> 01:02:33.410
I hereby deputize the both of you.

01:02:36.490 --> 01:02:39.770
This situation is resolved.

01:02:45.750 --> 01:02:49.180
Now, we've got to gather this fella in.

01:02:49.400 --> 01:02:52.840
I guess we've done just about all the talking we can do.

01:02:53.990 --> 01:02:56.620
So you shoot just as soon as you get a shot.

01:02:56.780 --> 01:02:57.540
I don't have a gun.

01:02:57.600 --> 01:02:59.460
You keep behind cover and you bring him down.

01:03:00.960 --> 01:03:05.830
I certainly wish I hadn't come to this with Calliope, but I guess he just went too far.

01:03:07.840 --> 01:03:10.440
It's up to Calliope to turn up his toes this time, I reckon.

01:03:11.600 --> 01:03:12.960
And don't be too reckless, boys.

01:03:13.180 --> 01:03:16.540
What Calliope shoots at, he hits.

01:03:16.800 --> 01:03:18.600
I don't have a gun.

01:03:36.330 --> 01:03:36.450
Thank you.

01:04:00.090 --> 01:04:06.250
But I tell you, I ain't going peacefully.

01:04:52.400 --> 01:04:52.740
I love you.

01:07:31.050 --> 01:07:32.190
Short straw stays.

01:07:38.270 --> 01:07:38.330
Oh.

01:07:43.610 --> 01:07:43.850
Yeah.

01:07:50.680 --> 01:07:51.340
That's it, then.

01:07:52.720 --> 01:07:57.110
Bill and I will go into the station, stay here, and cover the building in case he comes out.

01:07:58.880 --> 01:07:59.920
Oh, let me go in, bud.

01:08:01.080 --> 01:08:03.440
No, straws have been drawn, Tom.

01:08:06.160 --> 01:08:06.720
Come on, Bill.

01:08:07.520 --> 01:08:09.000
I don't have a gun.

01:08:11.160 --> 01:08:11.460
Dog.

01:08:13.160 --> 01:08:15.040
Does anybody else see what's wrong with this picture?

01:08:26.620 --> 01:08:29.370
Then the dog says, I don't have a gun.

01:08:31.470 --> 01:08:32.970
Oh, that's funny.

01:08:35.440 --> 01:08:36.660
Mr. King owns it?

01:08:37.440 --> 01:08:38.360
Where's Miss Gilmore?

01:08:38.700 --> 01:08:40.160
I haven't seen her since he left,

01:08:40.180 --> 01:08:43.080
and I've been looking through the Chronicles archives ever since,

01:08:43.440 --> 01:08:46.300
but I still haven't found anything that proves Mr. King wrong.

01:08:46.730 --> 01:08:48.030
I read the whole comic section.

01:08:48.190 --> 01:08:48.790
No help there.

01:08:49.210 --> 01:08:50.490
We've been trying to help, too.

01:08:51.570 --> 01:08:53.470
This is all we turned up in the library.

01:08:56.140 --> 01:08:57.000
That's my grandfather.

01:08:57.750 --> 01:08:59.070
Yeah, we thought he might know something.

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:01.620
I never thought of that. Let's go ask him.

01:09:01.740 --> 01:09:04.120
Hey, why don't you guys go see him and I'll keep looking in the archives.

01:09:04.420 --> 01:09:06.210
Good idea. Let me show you where they are.

01:09:08.360 --> 01:09:11.600
Joe, looks like we've been deputized to save the ranch.

01:09:18.580 --> 01:09:20.470
So, do you think he'll remember anything?

01:09:21.420 --> 01:09:24.080
I don't know. He usually can't remember what he had for breakfast.

01:09:38.090 --> 01:09:38.490
Grandpa?

01:09:40.290 --> 01:09:41.090
Who wants to know?

01:09:41.900 --> 01:09:42.720
It's me, Hank.

01:09:43.550 --> 01:09:43.830
Hank?

01:09:44.640 --> 01:09:45.260
Your grandson.

01:09:53.700 --> 01:09:54.560
Oh, that Hank!

01:09:57.640 --> 01:09:58.300
Would you?

01:09:58.880 --> 01:09:59.960
This is my friend, Joe Ta-

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:02.610
I meant the dog.

01:10:04.240 --> 01:10:05.570
Yeah, he is.

01:10:05.870 --> 01:10:06.770
This is Wishbone.

01:10:07.690 --> 01:10:08.610
He can wait by the bushes.

01:10:08.610 --> 01:10:09.250
He'll be okay.

01:10:09.780 --> 01:10:10.660
Hi there, little fella.

01:10:11.360 --> 01:10:12.100
Come on up here.

01:10:12.380 --> 01:10:13.220
I'll handle this, boys.

01:10:13.580 --> 01:10:14.660
Grandpa, it's like this.

01:10:15.170 --> 01:10:17.490
Mr. King claims that Wanda doesn't really own the Chronicle,

01:10:17.550 --> 01:10:19.890
and we were wondering if you could provide us with any information at all

01:10:19.890 --> 01:10:21.690
that could possibly help, or you could just scratch my head.

01:10:22.030 --> 01:10:24.870
We were just wondering if you knew anything about the Oakdale Chronicle.

01:10:25.980 --> 01:10:27.920
Of course I know the Oakdale Chronicle.

01:10:28.020 --> 01:10:28.620
It's the paper.

01:10:29.520 --> 01:10:29.980
We know.

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:40.720
Could you tell us what you remember about this picture? Do you remember anything? Yep, that's me. Right, Grandpa.

01:10:41.940 --> 01:10:50.160
We just wanted to know if Giles Gilmore really owned the paper. Of course he owned it. Owned it fair and square, didn't he, boy?

01:10:50.440 --> 01:10:59.170
Uh, sure. We remember, don't we, Jack? His name is Wishbone, sir. Right, you told me that.

01:11:00.160 --> 01:11:03.540
But I just can't tell you how much he reminds me of my dog, Jack.

01:11:05.200 --> 01:11:06.700
You look at this picture.

01:11:14.740 --> 01:11:15.340
Hidden image.

01:11:19.740 --> 01:11:21.740
How do you know it was Mr. Gilmore's fair and square?

01:11:22.510 --> 01:11:24.290
Look right there in your picture, Hank.

01:11:25.300 --> 01:11:25.640
Where?

01:11:27.370 --> 01:11:29.250
